      "result": " (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15976v1)\nciteturn6603view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 34; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15976v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1497\nL0@P0: TOD-ProcBench: Benchmarking Complex\nL1@P0: Instruction-Following in Task-Oriented Dialogues\nL2@P0: Sarik Ghazarian1 ∗ Abhinav Gullapalli1 ∗ † Swair Shah1 ‡ Anurag Beniwal1 ‡\nL3@P0: Nanyun Peng1 Narayanan Sadagopan1 Zhou Yu1 †\nL4@P0: 1Amazon\nL5@P0: {sghazari, gabhik, shahswai, beanurag, pengnany, sdgpn, amznzya}@amazon.com\nL6@P0: Abstract\nL7@P0: In real-world task-oriented dialogue (TOD) settings such as customer support for\nL8@P0: trip booking, banking, and healthcare, agents are required to strictly adhere to com\u0002plex instructions while conducting multi-turn conversations with customers. These\nL9@P0: instructions are typically presented in natural language format and include general\nL10@P0: guidelines and step-by-step procedures with complex constraints. Existing TOD\nL11@P0: benchmarks often oversimplify the complex nature of these instructions by reduc\u0002ing them to simple schemas composed of intents, slots, and API call configurations.\nL12@P0: To address this gap and systematically benchmark LLMs’ instruction-following\nL13@P0: capabilities, we propose TOD-ProcBench, a challenging benchmark featuring\nL14@P0: complex process instructions with intricate, fine-grained constraints that evalu\u0002ates various LLMs’ abilities to understand and follow instructions in multi-turn\nL15@P0: TODs. Our benchmark dataset comprises instruction documents derived from\nL16@P0: the high-quality ABCD dataset with corresponding conversations under human\nL17@P0: quality control. We formulate fine-grained constraints and action procedures\nL18@P0: as multi-level condition-action instruction statements. We design three tasks to\nL19@P0: comprehensively benchmark LLMs’ complex instruction-following capabilities in\nL20@P0: multi-turn TODs. Task 1 evaluates how LLMs retrieve the most relevant statement\nL21@P0: from a complex instruction and predict the corresponding next action. In Task\nL22@P0: 2, we synthesize instruction-violating responses by injecting inconsistencies and\nL23@P0: manipulating the original instructions, and then we analyze how effectively LLMs\nL24@P0: can identify instruction-violating responses. Task 3 investigates LLMs’ abilities\nL25@P0: in conditional generation of instruction-following responses based on the original\nL26@P0: complex instructions. Our benchmarking results reveal significant gaps in LLMs’\nL27@P0: instruction-following capabilities in multi-turn conversations across all three tasks.\nL28@P0: Additionally, we conduct studies on the impact of multilingual settings and differ\u0002ent instruction text formats on compliance performance. We release our bench\u0002mark under the Llama 3.3 Community License Agreement to advance research\nL29@P0: on multi-turn TODs’ complex instruction-following capabilities. The dataset is\nL30@P0: available for download at https://www.amazon.science/publications/tod-procbench\u0002benchmarking-complex-instruction-following-in-task-oriented-dialogues.\nL31@P0: ∗Equal contribution\nL32@P0: †Corresponding author\nL33@P0: ‡Work performed while at Amazon\nL34@P0: 39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025) Workshop: Multi-Turn Interactions\nL35@P0-1: in Large Language Models.1 Introduction\nL36@P1: Task-oriented dialogue systems (ToDs) take decisive steps and actions when interacting with users\nL37@P1: with the primary goal of satisfying customer requests [Wei et al., 2018, Sekulic et al., 2024, Xu\nL38@P1: et al., 2024]. In multi-turn interactions with users, they initially comprehend a user’s query and then\nL39@P1: collect information needed to address the request while following the constraints defined by complex\nL40@P1: instructions. Complex instructions comprise strict guidelines that dictate under what conditions the\nL41@P1: actions and API calls must be executed [Rastogi et al., 2020, Raimondo et al., 2023, Chen et al.,\nL42@P1: 2021, Roy et al., 2024]. The complexity of these instructions varies based on the user’s intent and the\nL43@P1: task domain.\nL44@P1: A: hi, what can i help you with?\nL45@P1: C: hi there. i wanted to check the status of my most recent order\nL46@P1: because i see an extra fee on the invoice, and i'm not sure why.\nL47@P1: A: okay, can I have your name?\nL48@P1: C: sure. my name is albert sanders\nL49@P1: Ac: account has been pulled up for albert sanders.\nL50@P1: A: can i have your account id and order id?\nL51@P1: C: my account id is <account_id> and my order id is <order_id>\nL52@P1: Ac: identity verification in progress ...\nL53@P1: Ac: querying the system for an answer ...\nL54@P1: A: what is your membership level?\nL55@P1: C: i have a guest membership\nL56@P1: pull-up-account\nL57@P1: verify-identity\nL58@P1: ask-the-oracle\nL59@P1: membership\nL60@P1: R1: i am sorry, this fee was not a mistake and i cannot remove it. -- Compliance score: 0.85\nL61@P1: R2: that's great, i will be able to remove the fee for you . -- Compliance score: 0.86\nL62@P1: R3: i am sorry, this fee was a mistake and i will remove it. -- Compliance score: 0.88\nL63@P1: If customer inquires about an extra/mystery fee on their order:\nL64@P1:  - Get customer's full name, account ID, and order ID to verify\nL65@P1: identity\nL66@P1:  - Check system for reason behind the extra fee\nL67@P1:  - If it was a company error:\nL68@P1:  - Ask customer's membership level\nL69@P1:  - If Gold or Silver member:\nL70@P1:  - Ask amount of extra fee\nL71@P1:  - Remove fee from order\nL72@P1:  - If Bronze or Guest member:\nL73@P1: - Explain fee cannot be removed\nL74@P1:  - Optionally offer to escalate to manager for Bronze members\nL75@P1:  - If not a company error (e.g. price surge, customer error):\nL76@P1:  - Explain reason for fee\nL77@P1:  - Do not remove fee unless Gold/Silver member as a courtesy\nL78@P1: update-order\nL79@P1: update-order\nL80@P1: Figure 1: Workflow steps (orange boxes) from ABCD dataset show the the order of calling actions\nL81@P1: for “status_mystery_fee” intent, an inadequate representation of all nuanced constraints in the\nL82@P1: corresponding TOD-ProcBench instruction document excerpt on the right. The compliance scores\nL83@P1: are computed with ComplianceScorer.\nL84@P1: Existing datasets either fail to capture challenges due to multi-turn conversation or fail to capture\nL85@P1: real-world instruction complexity, lacking fine-grained conditional instructions shown in Figure 1.\nL86@P1: Real-world instructions typically use natural language IF (Condition) - THEN (Action) structures\nL87@P1: which introduce ambiguity compared to symbolic formats but express more complex guidance. This\nL88@P1: creates a need for public datasets featuring multi-turn conversations that comply with fine-grained\nL89@P1: natural language conditional instructions. Figure 1 demonstrates how ComplianceScorer [Min et al.,\nL90@P1: 2023] incorrectly labels all responses as compliant when responses R2 and R3 actually violate fee\nL91@P1: removal instructions, highlighting the need for fine-grained conditional instructions beyond basic\nL92@P1: workflows. Natural language instructions offer the additional benefit of seamlessly sharing consistent\nL93@P1: instructions between human agents and chatbots without conversion. For this purpose, we construct a\nL94@P1: multilingual dataset called TOD-ProcBench which contains conversations and complex instructions\nL95@P1: in the condition-action format. Our contributions are:\nL96@P1: • We propose TOD-ProcBench, based on ABCD [Chen et al., 2021], as a collection of\nL97@P1: benchmarks with complex condition-action natural language instructions and multilingual\nL98@P1: multi-turn conversations.\nL99@P1: • We publish benchmarks for three TOD tasks: (1) instruction and next action selection -\nL100@P1: this task measures how well LLMs can identify applicable instructions during a multi-turn\nL101@P1: conversation (2) instruction-following evaluation - this task measures how well LLMs can\nL102@P1: detect violation of complex instruction (3) instruction-following response generation - this\nL103@P1: task measures how well LLMs can respond under complex instructions written for an entire\nL104@P1: conversational interaction.\nL105@P1: • We propose an approach for generating a test benchmark of instruction-violating responses\nL106@P1: in TOD-ProcBench by injecting different perturbations to instructions and prompting the\nL107@P1: LLM to generate responses which are similar to the ground-truth yet adhere to manipulated\nL108@P1: instructions.\nL109@P1-2: 2• We show that all LLMs considered in this study struggle to perform accurately on (1)\nL110@P2: complex formatted instruction selection, (2) instruction-violation response evaluation, and\nL111@P2: (3) instruction-following response generation tasks. In addition, we find that LLMs have\nL112@P2: similar multilingual capability for those tasks with a subtle preference for English.\nL113@P2: 2 Related Work\nL114@P2: In general, ToDs such as customer service chatbots, physical robots, virtual assistants [Cui et al., 2017,\nL115@P2: Sengupta et al., 2019, Lewis and White, 2023] are strictly required to comply with the predefined\nL116@P2: procedures when executing API calls and actions to resolve user issues. Datasets have been introduced\nL117@P2: to incorporate constraints on TODs through schema-guided paradigms. The schema of the synthetic\nL118@P2: SGD dataset [Rastogi et al., 2020] imposes constraints through the set of obligatory slots and the\nL119@P2: order in which they must be filled for actions to be executed. As shown in Appendix A, SGD schema\nL120@P2: for finding next flight intent includes slots (flight’s origin and destination, both airports, seating\nL121@P2: class and etc.), their definitions and values, and required slots for each intent. Similar to SGD,\nL122@P2: STAR [Mosig et al., 2020] and FLAP [Roy et al., 2024] present the schema concept in ToDs in\nL123@P2: flowchart format. For these datasets, the schema with actions and slot values do not usually reflect\nL124@P2: the nature of real-world instructions as the latter often has more complex step-by-step structure\nL125@P2: and condition-action combination. The Action-Based Conversations Dataset (ABCD) [Chen et al.,\nL126@P2: 2021] which our study is based on incorporates guidelines that a human agent needs to execute in a\nL127@P2: predefined order. We go a step further and present more realistic, natural language instructions in the\nL128@P2: form of multi-level condition-action statements that provide precise information about the conditions\nL129@P2: that are required to be true for actions to be executed. More recent datasets and studies benchmark\nL130@P2: an LLM agent’s ability to follow SOPs [Nandi et al., 2025, Li et al., 2025]. Their focus is more on\nL131@P2: the accuracy of tool selection alone, complementing our study of conversational capabilities in a\nL132@P2: multi-turn dialogue setting.\nL133@P2: 3 TOD-ProcBench\nL134@P2: 3.1 Problem Overview\nL135@P2: In our setting, to generate a response r for a given dialogue history u (for example, “user: i\nL136@P2: need to return this jacket. it is the wrong size”) within a conversation in language l, a TOD\nL137@P2: system D must follow a set of conditions c ⊆ C and execute a sequence of corresponding actions\nL138@P2: a = ⟨a1, a2, . . . , an⟩ with ai ∈ A outlined in a given instruction n ∈ N. We present multilingual\nL139@P2: TOD-ProcBench with two main dimensions having three and seven variations, respectively: (1) the\nL140@P2: format dimension f of n ∈ N with English natural language (f1=“Nested If-Then”, f2=“Flattened\nL141@P2: If-Then”, and f3=“Flattened JSON Mappings of Conditions and Actions”) and (2) the language\nL142@P2: dimension l of the conversation (Arabic, Chinese, English, French, German, Hindi, Spanish).\nL143@P2: 3.2 Complex Instruction Components\nL144@P2: 3.2.1 Conditions\nL145@P2: Specific states or attributes, such as customer attributes (e.g., membership level), item properties (e.g.,\nL146@P2: purchase date), or conversation states (e.g., user intent) must be verified before executing a sequence\nL147@P2: of corresponding actions.\nL148@P2: 3.2.2 Actions\nL149@P2: The TOD must execute specific operations or responses when associated conditions are satisfied.\nL150@P2: Actions are organized hierarchically with conditions. For example, when a user inquires about the\nL151@P2: return policy (1st-level condition), the agent must first identify the user’s membership level (1st-level\nL152@P2: action). If the user has a silver membership (2nd-level condition), then the agent provides specific\nL153@P2: information like “only purchases in the last 6 months are returnable” (2nd-level action).\nL154@P2-3: 33.2.3 Composition\nL155@P3: Similar to prior works such as Wen et al. [2024] and Li et al. [2025], we use six composition\nL156@P3: techniques to derive complete, complex instructions with the foundational condition and action\nL157@P3: components: Single, And, Or, Chain, Selection, Nesting.\nL158@P3: 3.3 Instruction Document Construction\nL159@P3: We derive TOD-ProcBench from the ABCD dataset [Chen et al., 2021], a collection of human\u0002written conversations created by annotators following predefined workflow actions. We aggregate all\nL160@P3: human-human conversations with the same user intent from ABCD, which inherently contain similar\nL161@P3: conversation trajectories. In Appendix B, we show examples and symbolic notations for each of the\nL162@P3: three complex instruction formats, f1, f2, f3.\nL163@P3: Format f1 We generated “Nested If-Then” complex instructions, with one distinct instruction\nL164@P3: document for each of the 55 user intents in ABCD, using LLMs with 1-shot examples for natural\nL165@P3: language condition-action instructions (averaging 146 conversations per intent). We quantify the\nL166@P3: complexity of TOD-ProcBench with characterizations such as an average conditional branching\nL167@P3: factor of 2.2 and up to 4 nested conditional levels. To answer the question “How does the format of\nL168@P3: complex natural language instructions affect an LLM’s compliance capability?”, we use f1 to derive\nL169@P3: two additional formats of the instructions which still present the fundamental conditions and actions\nL170@P3: in English natural language text.\nL171@P3: Format f2 “Flattened If-Then” effectively flattens the nested “If-Then” structure of f1 into a\nL172@P3: sequence of single-level condition-action “If-Then” statements. We use this format to evaluate\nL173@P3: whether LLMs have a strong preference for the Nesting and Chain composition techniques present\nL174@P3: in f1 but not f2. The flattened structure of f2 reduces a Chain into a series of composite conditions\nL175@P3: with And.\nL176@P3: Format f3 “Flattened JSON Mappings of Conditions and Actions” transforms the natural language\nL177@P3: “If-Then” statements in f2 into a structured JSON object containing a list of maps, each of which\nL178@P3: denotes relevant conditions and actions to be considered.\nL179@P3: 3.4 Expanding to Multilingual Conversations\nL180@P3: As a practical application of our work in constructing TOD-ProcBench and a first step to evaluate the\nL181@P3: multilingual capabilities of LLMs in complex instruction-following, we seek to answer the question\nL182@P3: “Are English instructions still effective to guide multi-turn conversations in another language?”. This\nL183@P3: study will help justify whether there is a need to produce a high-quality construction of instructions in\nL184@P3: other languages for production settings where English instructions may be used in global marketplaces.\nL185@P3: We translate only the multi-turn conversations component of the original ABCD dataset to six other\nL186@P3: diverse languages: Arabic, Chinese, French, German, Hindi, Spanish.\nL187@P3: 3.5 Quality Verification\nL188@P3: We ask human annotators to assess the quality of TOD-ProcBench’s English conversation pair\u0002ings with the 55 generated documents in format f1 for the test conversations of ABCD dataset.\nL189@P3: The annotators answer the following Yes/No questions shown in the user interface in Figure 3 in\nL190@P3: Appendix A:\nL191@P3: Accuracy: Does the generated instruction accurately reflect information in the conversation? If the\nL192@P3: answer is no, the annotator should provide which part of the instruction is incorrect.\nL193@P3: Missing: Is there any important information from the conversation that is missing from the model\u0002generated instruction? If the answer is yes, the annotator should specify which part from the\nL194@P3: conversation is not in the instruction.\nL195@P3: Three annotators evaluated each conversation-instruction pair (3,012 total annotations). Annota\u0002tor agreement was complete for 63% of conversations, with majority voting determining “accu\u0002racy” (82%) and “missing” (15%) assessments. The top-3 accurate annotations are for the re\u0002cover_password, recover_username and search_results intents which share similar conversation\nL196@P3-4: 4trajectories, while the bottom-3 belong to the shirt, timing and policy intents which can include a\nL197@P4: broad range of queries. We only focus on the 769 high-quality pairs. Using English conversation\nL198@P4: pairings with English instructions in format f1 as a base version of TOD-ProcBench, we expand the\nL199@P4: dataset along the two dimensions of instruction format and conversation language. For the format\nL200@P4: and multilingual expansion datasets, we leverage the LLM judge to evaluate the quality of both, with\nL201@P4: 100% correctness for the format changes and 98.3% to 99.8% for each language variation.\nL202@P4: 4 Tasks\nL203@P4: For all experiments across three tasks, three instruction formats, and seven conversation languages,\nL204@P4: we prompt the following six LLMs to conduct chain-of-thought (CoT) [Wu et al., 2023] by first\nL205@P4: generating a step-by-step reasoning and then generate the respective output: Qwen3-14B [Yang\nL206@P4: et al., 2025], Llama3.3-70B 4, Gemma3-27B-IT 5, Claude3.5-Sonnet-V1 6, Claude3.5-Sonnet-V2 7,\nL207@P4: Claude3.7-Sonnet 8. Experimental results which are not presented in tables in the following sections\nL208@P4: are presented in Appendix D for completeness.\nL209@P4: 4.1 Task 1: Instruction Retrieval and Next Action Prediction\nL210@P4: Task 1 evaluates ToDs’ ability to retrieve relevant instructions and predict appropriate next actions\nL211@P4: conditioned on conversation history. For all 30 actions (details in Appendix A, C) from the ABCD\nL212@P4: dataset, we define trigger conditions that determine when each action should be executed. We also\nL213@P4: introduce an empty action to handle scenarios in which the customer’s goal has already been met and\nL214@P4: no further action is required from the agent.\nL215@P4: 4.1.1 Dataset\nL216@P4: To evaluate model performance in Task 1 at different stages of the user-agent interaction, we create\nL217@P4: input data by extracting all partial conversation transcripts that end with the customer’s turn in a\nL218@P4: given conversation. For example, a conversation with four customer turns leads to a list of four\nL219@P4: partial conversations, each ending with a customer turn. We then pair each partial conversation\nL220@P4: with its corresponding instruction. Overall, 6953 partial conversations are extracted from 1004 test\nL221@P4: conversations.\nL222@P4: In order to examine the accuracy of models’ predictions for the agent’s next action, we process the list\nL223@P4: of workflow steps from ABCD for each partial conversation history and identify actions that are most\nL224@P4: relevant to the existing agent turns in the conversation history. The first action from the remaining\nL225@P4: workflow steps is used as the ground-truth next agent action. Examples of input data for Task1 are\nL226@P4: shown in Appendix A.\nL227@P4: 4.1.2 Results\nL228@P4: We prompt six LLMs to generate the the next agent turn and top k = 1 or k = 2 most relevant\nL229@P4: instruction given the conversation history, list of all instructions and possible agent actions with\nL230@P4: the conditions for invoking each of these actions. In computing the top k = 2 accuracy, we check\nL231@P4: whether any of the two relevant instructions retrieved by LLMs is the ground-truth instruction or not.\nL232@P4: All models are executed in a few-shot in-context learning [Brown et al., 2020] paradigm where we\nL233@P4: provide three demonstrations for LLMs.\nL234@P4: Table 1 depicts the accuracy of the models in predicting the correct instruction and agent’s next\nL235@P4: action. While the larger LLMs maintain a relatively more consistent performance for k = 1 and\nL236@P4: k = 2, all LLMs recorded higher accuracy for the top-k = 2 scenario for both overall accuracy\nL237@P4: and only instruction retrieval. Most LLMs demonstrate a preference for format f1 and all LLMs\nL238@P4: demonstrate more difficulty in correctly predicting the agent’s next action, likely attributable to the\nL239@P4: subtle distinctions between actions and the overlapping nature of their triggering conditions. We also\nL240@P4: 4\nL241@P4: https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct\nL242@P4: 5\nL243@P4: https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-3-27b-it\nL244@P4: 6\nL245@P4: https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-3-5-sonnet\nL246@P4: 7\nL247@P4: https://www.anthropic.com/news/3-5-models-and-computer-use\nL248@P4: 8\nL249@P4: https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-3-7-sonnet\nL250@P4-5: 5Model Overall Instruction Retrieval Begin Middle End Best Format\nL251@P5: Claude3.7-Sonnet 0.3948 / 0.4310 0.8267 / 0.8943 0.3878 / 0.4481 0.3834 / 0.4074 0.4161 / 0.4359 f2 / f1\nL252@P5: Claude3.5-Sonnet-V2 0.3312 / 0.4182 0.7311 / 0.8765 0.3270 / 0.4214 0.3333 / 0.4126 0.3341 / 0.4204 f1 / f3\nL253@P5: Claude3.5-Sonnet-V1 0.3187 / 0.3716 0.7057 / 0.8304 0.3185 / 0.3750 0.3342 / 0.3730 0.3018 / 0.3660 f3 / f3\nL254@P5: Qwen3-14B 0.3122 / 0.3279 0.7792 / 0.8419 0.1645 / 0.1668 0.2828 / 0.2880 0.5289 / 0.5728 f1 / f1\nL255@P5: Gemma3-27B-IT 0.2221 / 0.2559 0.6138 / 0.7559 0.0770 / 0.0755 0.2144 / 0.2392 0.4113 / 0.4990 f1 / f2\nL256@P5: Llama3.3-70B 0.1447 / 0.2497 0.2503 / 0.6612 0.1463 / 0.2620 0.1477 / 0.2366 0.1393 / 0.2488 f2 / f1\nL257@P5: Table 1: Task 1 accuracy and best performing instruction format for top k = 1 and k = 2 instruction\nL258@P5: retrieval and next agent action prediction given English conversations. Each entry is given in the form\nL259@P5: k = 1/k = 2. “Overall” denotes both instruction retrieval and next action prediction as opposed to\nL260@P5: only “Instruction Retrieval”. “Begin”, “Middle”, and “End” denote the respective 1/3 segments of the\nL261@P5: conversation. The best result per column is bolded.\nL262@P5: Language Claude3.7-Sonnet Claude3.5-Sonnet-V2 Claude3.5-Sonnet-V1 Qwen3-14B Gemma3-27B-IT Llama3.3-70B\nL263@P5: EN 0.3959/0.3932 0.3270/0.3312 0.3019/0.2963 0.1645/0.3122 0.0770/0.2221 0.1374/0.1358\nL264@P5: FR 0.4141/0.4041 0.3615/0.3613 0.3522/0.3299 0.1540/0.2976 0.0441/0.1877 0.1188/0.1146\nL265@P5: DE 0.3885/0.3896 0.3483/0.3524 0.3386/0.3283 0.1242/0.2786 0.0708/0.2088 0.1262/0.1126\nL266@P5: ES 0.3955/0.4005 0.3518/0.3472 0.3282/0.3268 0.1521/0.2874 0.0658/0.1978 0.1327/0.1280\nL267@P5: ZH 0.3808/0.3768 0.3212/0.3209 0.3185/0.3216 0.1521/0.2983 0.0550/0.1921 0.0851/0.0840\nL268@P5: AR 0.3820/0.3778 0.3007/0.3036 0.3293/0.3206 0.1726/0.2934 0.0433/0.1585 0.0298/0.0299\nL269@P5: HI 0.3785/0.3882 0.3317/0.3141 0.3386/0.3365 0.1718/0.3017 0.0701/0.1878 0.0252/0.0240\nL270@P5: Table 2: Task 1 accuracy for predicting the correct next action and retrieving the correct top k = 1\nL271@P5: instruction in the beginning 1/3 of the conversation (Begin) and for every agent response (Overall),\nL272@P5: denoted by Begin/Overall entries in the table. Results are shown across models and conversation\nL273@P5: languages (English (EN), French (FR), German (DE), Spanish (ES), Chinese (ZH), Arabic (AR),\nL274@P5: Hindi (HI)). The best result per language is bolded and the best result per model is underlined.\nL275@P5: compute accuracy of the models in predicting the correct instruction and action based on relative\nL276@P5: position of the given agent response in the conversation, assigning turns to beginning, middle or end\nL277@P5: based on whether they are located in the beginning, middle, or final third of the agent turns in the\nL278@P5: conversation. According to Table 1, larger models perform consistently throughout the conversation.\nL279@P5: Although smaller models perform better at instruction retrieval and action predictions as they get\nL280@P5: more information from the conversation history, all LLMs still have more room for improvement.\nL281@P5: As shown in Appendix D, larger models tend to outperform smaller models across the conversation\nL282@P5: languages but performance of all LLMs is not severely impacted by language.\nL283@P5: 4.2 Task 2: Compliance Evaluation\nL284@P5: The main goal of Task 2 is to benchmark LLMs’ capability on scoring the compliance of a given ToD’s\nL285@P5: response with respect to the dialogue history and the instruction that the agent must follow. Such\nL286@P5: capability is essential to be used as both a quality verifier during inference and a proxy for improving\nL287@P5: the development process for instruction-following ToDs. We formulate a binary classification\nL288@P5: task where the goal is to distinguish between instruction-following and instruction-violating agent\nL289@P5: responses.\nL290@P5: Dataset For the compliance evaluation task, we only focus on agent utterances that are directly\nL291@P5: relevant to instructions. To isolate such responses from generic responses such as greeting / acknowl\u0002edging customer / pure empathy, we leverage a LLM to classify agent turns as relevant or irrelevant\nL292@P5: in the 6953 partial conversations from Task 1 dataset.\nL293@P5: Compliance Manipulation Pipeline To build our binary instruction-following / instruction\u0002violating dataset, we use human-written ABCD responses as instruction-following examples and\nL294@P5: generate instruction-violating counterparts through a four-step pipeline (Figure 2): (1) extract relevant\nL295@P5: instruction sections, (2) manipulate the extracted instructions, (3) generate instruction-violating re\u0002sponses based on the manipulated instructions, (4) verify whether the generated instruction-violating\nL296@P5: response entails the original ground-truth response.\nL297@P5: 1) Instruction Section Extraction: We use dialogue history, instruction document, and the ground\u0002truth instruction-following agent response as input when prompting the LLM to select a section\nL298@P5: (including its closest ‘if’ condition) of the instruction to which the ground-truth response is directly\nL299@P5: related. If such a relevant part of the instruction is not present, we exclude this response sample.\nL300@P5-6: 6Figure 2: The proposed pipeline for generating instruction-violating responses for Task 2.\nL301@P6: Model EN FR DE ES ZH AR HI\nL302@P6: Claude3.7-Sonnet 0.7608/0.7298 0.7558/0.7233 0.7533/0.7260 0.7490/0.7283 0.7407/0.7170 0.7510/0.7205 0.7472/0.7152\nL303@P6: Claude3.5-Sonnet-V2 0.7215/0.6874 0.7253/0.6776 0.7187/0.6763 0.7207/0.6887 0.7175/0.6640 0.7114/0.6582 0.7033/0.6524\nL304@P6: Claude3.5-Sonnet-V1 0.6847/0.6854 0.6867/0.6811 0.6869/0.6784 0.6834/0.6796 0.6615/0.6604 0.6483/0.6551 0.6435/0.6496\nL305@P6: Llama3.3-70B 0.7283/0.5636 0.7273/0.5346 0.7303/0.5388 0.7250/0.5356 0.6978/0.5545 0.6266/0.4970 0.6405/0.5129\n"
