      "result": " (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15976v1)\nciteturn6604view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 34; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6603view0\",\"lineno\":306}); Total lines: 1497\nL214@P4: no further action is required from the agent.\nL215@P4: 4.1.1 Dataset\nL216@P4: To evaluate model performance in Task 1 at different stages of the user-agent interaction, we create\nL217@P4: input data by extracting all partial conversation transcripts that end with the customer’s turn in a\nL218@P4: given conversation. For example, a conversation with four customer turns leads to a list of four\nL219@P4: partial conversations, each ending with a customer turn. We then pair each partial conversation\nL220@P4: with its corresponding instruction. Overall, 6953 partial conversations are extracted from 1004 test\nL221@P4: conversations.\nL222@P4: In order to examine the accuracy of models’ predictions for the agent’s next action, we process the list\nL223@P4: of workflow steps from ABCD for each partial conversation history and identify actions that are most\nL224@P4: relevant to the existing agent turns in the conversation history. The first action from the remaining\nL225@P4: workflow steps is used as the ground-truth next agent action. Examples of input data for Task1 are\nL226@P4: shown in Appendix A.\nL227@P4: 4.1.2 Results\nL228@P4: We prompt six LLMs to generate the the next agent turn and top k = 1 or k = 2 most relevant\nL229@P4: instruction given the conversation history, list of all instructions and possible agent actions with\nL230@P4: the conditions for invoking each of these actions. In computing the top k = 2 accuracy, we check\nL231@P4: whether any of the two relevant instructions retrieved by LLMs is the ground-truth instruction or not.\nL232@P4: All models are executed in a few-shot in-context learning [Brown et al., 2020] paradigm where we\nL233@P4: provide three demonstrations for LLMs.\nL234@P4: Table 1 depicts the accuracy of the models in predicting the correct instruction and agent’s next\nL235@P4: action. While the larger LLMs maintain a relatively more consistent performance for k = 1 and\nL236@P4: k = 2, all LLMs recorded higher accuracy for the top-k = 2 scenario for both overall accuracy\nL237@P4: and only instruction retrieval. Most LLMs demonstrate a preference for format f1 and all LLMs\nL238@P4: demonstrate more difficulty in correctly predicting the agent’s next action, likely attributable to the\nL239@P4: subtle distinctions between actions and the overlapping nature of their triggering conditions. We also\nL240@P4: 4\nL241@P4: https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct\nL242@P4: 5\nL243@P4: https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-3-27b-it\nL244@P4: 6\nL245@P4: https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-3-5-sonnet\nL246@P4: 7\nL247@P4: https://www.anthropic.com/news/3-5-models-and-computer-use\nL248@P4: 8\nL249@P4: https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-3-7-sonnet\nL250@P4-5: 5Model Overall Instruction Retrieval Begin Middle End Best Format\nL251@P5: Claude3.7-Sonnet 0.3948 / 0.4310 0.8267 / 0.8943 0.3878 / 0.4481 0.3834 / 0.4074 0.4161 / 0.4359 f2 / f1\nL252@P5: Claude3.5-Sonnet-V2 0.3312 / 0.4182 0.7311 / 0.8765 0.3270 / 0.4214 0.3333 / 0.4126 0.3341 / 0.4204 f1 / f3\nL253@P5: Claude3.5-Sonnet-V1 0.3187 / 0.3716 0.7057 / 0.8304 0.3185 / 0.3750 0.3342 / 0.3730 0.3018 / 0.3660 f3 / f3\nL254@P5: Qwen3-14B 0.3122 / 0.3279 0.7792 / 0.8419 0.1645 / 0.1668 0.2828 / 0.2880 0.5289 / 0.5728 f1 / f1\nL255@P5: Gemma3-27B-IT 0.2221 / 0.2559 0.6138 / 0.7559 0.0770 / 0.0755 0.2144 / 0.2392 0.4113 / 0.4990 f1 / f2\nL256@P5: Llama3.3-70B 0.1447 / 0.2497 0.2503 / 0.6612 0.1463 / 0.2620 0.1477 / 0.2366 0.1393 / 0.2488 f2 / f1\nL257@P5: Table 1: Task 1 accuracy and best performing instruction format for top k = 1 and k = 2 instruction\nL258@P5: retrieval and next agent action prediction given English conversations. Each entry is given in the form\nL259@P5: k = 1/k = 2. “Overall” denotes both instruction retrieval and next action prediction as opposed to\nL260@P5: only “Instruction Retrieval”. “Begin”, “Middle”, and “End” denote the respective 1/3 segments of the\nL261@P5: conversation. The best result per column is bolded.\nL262@P5: Language Claude3.7-Sonnet Claude3.5-Sonnet-V2 Claude3.5-Sonnet-V1 Qwen3-14B Gemma3-27B-IT Llama3.3-70B\nL263@P5: EN 0.3959/0.3932 0.3270/0.3312 0.3019/0.2963 0.1645/0.3122 0.0770/0.2221 0.1374/0.1358\nL264@P5: FR 0.4141/0.4041 0.3615/0.3613 0.3522/0.3299 0.1540/0.2976 0.0441/0.1877 0.1188/0.1146\nL265@P5: DE 0.3885/0.3896 0.3483/0.3524 0.3386/0.3283 0.1242/0.2786 0.0708/0.2088 0.1262/0.1126\nL266@P5: ES 0.3955/0.4005 0.3518/0.3472 0.3282/0.3268 0.1521/0.2874 0.0658/0.1978 0.1327/0.1280\nL267@P5: ZH 0.3808/0.3768 0.3212/0.3209 0.3185/0.3216 0.1521/0.2983 0.0550/0.1921 0.0851/0.0840\nL268@P5: AR 0.3820/0.3778 0.3007/0.3036 0.3293/0.3206 0.1726/0.2934 0.0433/0.1585 0.0298/0.0299\nL269@P5: HI 0.3785/0.3882 0.3317/0.3141 0.3386/0.3365 0.1718/0.3017 0.0701/0.1878 0.0252/0.0240\nL270@P5: Table 2: Task 1 accuracy for predicting the correct next action and retrieving the correct top k = 1\nL271@P5: instruction in the beginning 1/3 of the conversation (Begin) and for every agent response (Overall),\nL272@P5: denoted by Begin/Overall entries in the table. Results are shown across models and conversation\nL273@P5: languages (English (EN), French (FR), German (DE), Spanish (ES), Chinese (ZH), Arabic (AR),\nL274@P5: Hindi (HI)). The best result per language is bolded and the best result per model is underlined.\nL275@P5: compute accuracy of the models in predicting the correct instruction and action based on relative\nL276@P5: position of the given agent response in the conversation, assigning turns to beginning, middle or end\nL277@P5: based on whether they are located in the beginning, middle, or final third of the agent turns in the\nL278@P5: conversation. According to Table 1, larger models perform consistently throughout the conversation.\nL279@P5: Although smaller models perform better at instruction retrieval and action predictions as they get\nL280@P5: more information from the conversation history, all LLMs still have more room for improvement.\nL281@P5: As shown in Appendix D, larger models tend to outperform smaller models across the conversation\nL282@P5: languages but performance of all LLMs is not severely impacted by language.\nL283@P5: 4.2 Task 2: Compliance Evaluation\nL284@P5: The main goal of Task 2 is to benchmark LLMs’ capability on scoring the compliance of a given ToD’s\nL285@P5: response with respect to the dialogue history and the instruction that the agent must follow. Such\nL286@P5: capability is essential to be used as both a quality verifier during inference and a proxy for improving\nL287@P5: the development process for instruction-following ToDs. We formulate a binary classification\nL288@P5: task where the goal is to distinguish between instruction-following and instruction-violating agent\nL289@P5: responses.\nL290@P5: Dataset For the compliance evaluation task, we only focus on agent utterances that are directly\nL291@P5: relevant to instructions. To isolate such responses from generic responses such as greeting / acknowl\u0002edging customer / pure empathy, we leverage a LLM to classify agent turns as relevant or irrelevant\nL292@P5: in the 6953 partial conversations from Task 1 dataset.\nL293@P5: Compliance Manipulation Pipeline To build our binary instruction-following / instruction\u0002violating dataset, we use human-written ABCD responses as instruction-following examples and\nL294@P5: generate instruction-violating counterparts through a four-step pipeline (Figure 2): (1) extract relevant\nL295@P5: instruction sections, (2) manipulate the extracted instructions, (3) generate instruction-violating re\u0002sponses based on the manipulated instructions, (4) verify whether the generated instruction-violating\nL296@P5: response entails the original ground-truth response.\nL297@P5: 1) Instruction Section Extraction: We use dialogue history, instruction document, and the ground\u0002truth instruction-following agent response as input when prompting the LLM to select a section\nL298@P5: (including its closest ‘if’ condition) of the instruction to which the ground-truth response is directly\nL299@P5: related. If such a relevant part of the instruction is not present, we exclude this response sample.\nL300@P5-6: 6Figure 2: The proposed pipeline for generating instruction-violating responses for Task 2.\nL301@P6: Model EN FR DE ES ZH AR HI\nL302@P6: Claude3.7-Sonnet 0.7608/0.7298 0.7558/0.7233 0.7533/0.7260 0.7490/0.7283 0.7407/0.7170 0.7510/0.7205 0.7472/0.7152\nL303@P6: Claude3.5-Sonnet-V2 0.7215/0.6874 0.7253/0.6776 0.7187/0.6763 0.7207/0.6887 0.7175/0.6640 0.7114/0.6582 0.7033/0.6524\nL304@P6: Claude3.5-Sonnet-V1 0.6847/0.6854 0.6867/0.6811 0.6869/0.6784 0.6834/0.6796 0.6615/0.6604 0.6483/0.6551 0.6435/0.6496\nL305@P6: Llama3.3-70B 0.7283/0.5636 0.7273/0.5346 0.7303/0.5388 0.7250/0.5356 0.6978/0.5545 0.6266/0.4970 0.6405/0.5129\nL306@P6: Qwen3-14B 0.7046/0.6937 0.6968/0.6905 0.6958/0.6872 0.6965/0.6847 0.6965/0.6907 0.6920/0.6837 0.6895/0.6758\nL307@P6: Gemma3-27B-IT 0.6968/0.5616 0.6890/0.5436 0.6773/0.5477 0.6935/0.5482 0.6890/0.5404 0.6791/0.5376 0.6804/0.5409\nL308@P6: Table 3: Task 2 accuracy comparison between Approach 1/Approach 2 with instruction format f1\nL309@P6: across conversation languages (English (EN), French (FR), German (DE), Spanish (ES), Chinese\nL310@P6: (ZH), Arabic (AR), Hindi (HI)). The best result per language is bolded and the best result per model\nL311@P6: is underlined.\nL312@P6: 2) Instruction Manipulation: We apply systematic manipulations to both the extracted instruction\nL313@P6: section and the complete source instruction using an LLM to simulate the following categories of\nL314@P6: violations:\nL315@P6: • Fine-grained parameter mismatch: We substitute numerical values (e.g., number of days\nL316@P6: for which a customer is eligible to return an item) in the extracted section of the instruction\nL317@P6: with randomly generated numbers, simulating subtle compliance violations.\nL318@P6: • Single-condition action replacement: For instructions containing one condition with\nL319@P6: multiple actions, we randomly substitute an original action with either its negation or an\nL320@P6: alternative action from the same instruction, capturing action-level instruction violations.\nL321@P6: • Multi-condition action replacement: For instructions with a composition of multiple\nL322@P6: conditions, we alter the logical condition-action relationships by randomly replacing a target\nL323@P6: action with an action corresponding to other conditional branches within the same instruction.\nL324@P6: For example, we could apply standard member return policies for a gold member, violating\nL325@P6: the true dependence of actions on their corresponding conditions.\nL326@P6: 3) Response Generation: We provide the dialogue history, the manipulated and original instructions,\nL327@P6: and the ground-truth response to the LLM and prompt it to minimally revise the ground-truth response\nL328@P6: such that it covers the main difference between the original and manipulated instruction. We explain\nL329@P6: in the prompt that the generated response must comply with the manipulated instruction. This\nL330@P6: implicitly guarantees that the generated response is an instruction-violating response.\nL331@P6: We used LLM to verify that generated instruction-violating responses do not entail ground-truth\nL332@P6: responses. Of 2288 generated samples, 307 required additional manipulation cycles. Manual\nL333@P6: verification confirmed 87% accuracy in instruction violations (1982 samples), resulting in a final\nL334@P6: dataset of 3964 balanced examples (compliant and non-compliant) for Task 2.\nL335@P6: Results We compare the six LLMs as binary classifiers for instruction violations in TOD-ProcBench\nL336@P6: using two approaches:\nL337@P6: • Approach 1: Given the conversation, next agent response, and relevant instruction section,\nL338@P6: the LLM should classify the agent’s next response as compliant or instruction-violating.\nL339@P6: • Approach 2: LLM first attributes the given agent response to the most relevant instruction\nL340@P6: and then verifies whether the identified instruction and originally provided ground-truth\nL341@P6: instruction are entailing each other. Entailment implies a compliant response.\nL342@P6: We leverage accuracy as the evaluation metric for Task 2. As Table 3 indicates, most models perform\nL343@P6: slightly better for English conversations in both approaches. Table 3 also highlights Approach 2’s\nL344@P6: impact on the compliance evaluation performance as all models demonstrate inferior performance\nL345@P6-7: 7when relying on instruction-level comparisons. Approach 2 increases the complexity of the compli\u0002ance evaluation task by having the model first generate the relevant instruction based on the given\nL346@P7: conversation and then verify compliance with the generated instruction. Table 32 in the Appendix\nL347@P7: also demonstrates that the LLMs are unable to conduct the compliance evaluation task accurately and\nL348@P7: have a subtle preference for format f1, calling for further studies.\nL349@P7: 4.3 Task 3: Compliant Response Generation\nL350@P7: Inspired by real-world scenarios of conditional generations [Chen et al., 2019, Siddique et al., 2022,\nL351@P7: Narayan et al., 2023], in Task 3, we investigate ToDs’ conditional generation abilities based on our\nL352@P7: predefined instructions which require responses to follow the specified guidelines.\nL353@P7: Dataset In Task 3, we only examine TODs’ abilities to generate follow-up responses given that\nL354@P7: the previous conversation is already compliant with given instructions. We use the 3310 partial\nL355@P7: conversations from the first step of the Task2 pipeline containing instruction-following responses,\nL356@P7: dialogue histories, and corresponding instructions.\nL357@P7: Results We instruct the six LLMs to continue the conversation while following the given instruction.\nL358@P7: In the prompt, we decompose the problem to identify the steps the agent has followed, select the\nL359@P7: next most relevant action based on the given instruction, and generate a response that matches\nL360@P7: the instruction’s retrieved action. We employ Claude3.7-Sonnet (the top-performing Task 2 LLM)\nL361@P7: as a judge to assess response quality and compliance without ground-truth knowledge. Using its\nL362@P7: compliance classifications, we calculate compliance rates for Task 3, where higher scores indicate\nL363@P7: better consistent adherence to instructions.\nL364@P7: Model EN FR DE ES ZH AR HI\nL365@P7: Claude3.7-Sonnet 0.9529 0.9553 0.9535 0.9628 0.9616 0.9680 0.9622\nL366@P7: Claude3.5-Sonnet-V2 0.9532 0.9505 0.9432 0.9544 0.9529 0.9559 0.9505\nL367@P7: Claude3.5-Sonnet-V1 0.9254 0.9353 0.9344 0.9378 0.9375 0.9429 0.9390\nL368@P7: Gemma3-27B-IT 0.8952 0.3130 0.3121 0.3009 0.3205 0.3278 0.3323\nL369@P7: Llama3.3-70B 0.1435 0.4773 0.5773 0.3565 0.5227 0.3931 0.3834\nL370@P7: Qwen3-14B 0.2269 0.2281 0.2254 0.2142 0.2326 0.2369 0.2375\nL371@P7: Table 4: Task 3 compliance rate across models and conversation languages (English (EN), French\nL372@P7: (FR), German (DE), Spanish (ES), Chinese (ZH), Arabic (AR), Hindi (HI)) with instruction format\nL373@P7: f1. The best result per language is bolded and the best result per model is underlined.\nL374@P7: Table 4 compares the quality of the models’ generations using prior criteria from the given instruc\u0002tions. Larger LLMs tend to exhibit higher compliance in comparison to smaller models, across the\nL375@P7: conversation language and instruction format dimensions. Some smaller models tend to not respond\nL376@P7: in the specified response format, leading to many non-compliant responses and very low compliance\nL377@P7: rates. The performance of all LLMs is not significantly impacted by the language. We observe that\nL378@P7: the models usually generate longer utterances as it is hard for them to accurately identify one next\nL379@P7: action from the instruction and to exclusively cover that action. Even though the instruction-following\nL380@P7: generations may not be semantically close to ground-truth responses, Claude3.7-Sonnet still considers\nL381@P7: them as compliant.\nL382@P7: 5 Conclusion\nL383@P7: In this work, we apply complex condition-action formatted natural language instructions for ToDs to\nL384@P7: closely model the complexity of real world complex instructions. With these instruction documents\nL385@P7: and their application to multilingual conversations, we propose three related tasks: instruction retrieval\nL386@P7: and next action prediction, instruction-following evaluation, and compliant response generation. We\nL387@P7: publish benchmarks for each task in TOD-ProcBench and show that advanced LLMs still strive\nL388@P7: to perform admirably in all of these tasks and with enhanced multilingual capabilities for more\nL389@P7: challenging, fine-grained tasks. As future works, we plan to extend TOD-ProcBench to additional\nL390@P7: dataset formats including codified structures across domain-specific instructions.\nL391@P7: Acknowledgments\nL392@P7: We would like to thank Leo Feldman for his invaluable guidance and support in helping us adapt a\nL393@P7: system that he built for efficient LLM inference.\nL394@P7-8: 8References\nL395@P8: Tom Brown, Benjamin Mann, Nick Ryder, Melanie Subbiah, Jared D Kaplan, Prafulla Dhariwal,\nL396@P8: Arvind Neelakantan, Pranav Shyam, Girish Sastry, Amanda Askell, et al. Language models are\nL397@P8: few-shot learners. Advances in neural information processing systems, 33, 2020.\nL398@P8: Derek Chen, Howard Chen, Yi Yang, Alexander Lin, and Zhou Yu. Action-based conversations\nL399@P8: dataset: A corpus for building more in-depth task-oriented dialogue systems. In Kristina Toutanova,\nL400@P8: Anna Rumshisky, Luke Zettlemoyer, Dilek Hakkani-Tur, Iz Beltagy, Steven Bethard, Ryan\nL401@P8: Cotterell, Tanmoy Chakraborty, and Yichao Zhou, editors, Proceedings of the 2021 Confer\u0002ence of the NAACL: Human Language Technologies, pages 3002–3017, Online, June 2021.\nL402@P8: Association for Computational Linguistics. doi: 10.18653/v1/2021.naacl-main.239. URL\nL403@P8: https://aclanthology.org/2021.naacl-main.239.\nL404@P8: Wenhu Chen, Jianshu Chen, Pengda Qin, Xifeng Yan, and William Yang Wang. Semanti\u0002cally conditioned dialog response generation via hierarchical disentangled self-attention. In\nL405@P8: Anna Korhonen, David Traum, and Lluís Màrquez, editors, Proceedings of the 57th Annual\nL406@P8: Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics, pages 3696–3709, Florence, Italy,\nL407@P8: July 2019. Association for Computational Linguistics. doi: 10.18653/v1/P19-1360. URL\nL408@P8: https://aclanthology.org/P19-1360.\nL409@P8: Lei Cui, Shaohan Huang, Furu Wei, Chuanqi Tan, Chaoqun Duan, and Ming Zhou. SuperAgent:\nL410@P8: A customer service chatbot for E-commerce websites. In Mohit Bansal and Heng Ji, editors,\nL411@P8: Proceedings of ACL 2017, System Demonstrations, Vancouver, Canada, July 2017. Association for\nL412@P8: Computational Linguistics. URL https://aclanthology.org/P17-4017.\nL413@P8: Ashley Lewis and Michael White. Mitigating harms of llms via knowledge distillation for a virtual\nL414@P8: museum tour guide. In Proceedings of the 1st Workshop on Taming Large Language Models:\nL415@P8: Controllability in the era of Interactive Assistants!, 2023.\nL416@P8: Zekun Li, Shinda Huang, Jiangtian Wang, Nathan Zhang, Antonis Antoniades, Wenyue Hua, Kaijie\nL417@P8: Zhu, Sirui Zeng, Chi Wang, William Yang Wang, and Xifeng Yan. Sopbench: Evaluating\nL418@P8: language agents at following standa